  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nRain persists early this afternoon for parts of northeast Minnesota \nassociated with the warm air advection area ahead of a low pressure \nsystem that will pass across the Canadian Border into this evening. \nAs we go into this afternoon, an area of clearing in west-central \nMinnesota will promote some surface-based instability. A cold front \npassing through later this afternoon and evening should kick off \nadditional showers and thunderstorms. Convective parameters don't \nlook favorable for strong/severe storms with MUCAPE likely around \n1000 J/kg and rather straight hodographs. We could have some gusty \nwinds with the storms, but otherwise expecting them to be pretty run-\nof-the-mill thunderstorms. These should pass through along with the \nfront through this evening, with the threat ending just after \nmidnight. \n\nCold air advection and a little moisture remaining could \nkeep some sprinkles or light scattered showers around through Sunday \nmorning. With dry northwest flow on Sunday, shower activity looks \nlike it should decrease by midday, but it's not totally out of the \nrealm of possibility that there could be a few pop-up showers Sunday \nafternoon if there is just enough moisture remaining. Otherwise, I \nlowered RH a bit from NBM Sunday afternoon as northwest flow has \nbeen producing pretty dry RH lately given wildfire influence and \njust general dry air masses.\n\nHigh pressure will pass through Sunday night into Monday, leading to \nquiet weather. Winds will be more southwest on Monday and with \nlingering dry air, RH may dip pretty low. I lowered RH below NBM \nagain Monday afternoon with some 30-40% min RH values not out of the \nquestion. \n\nA passing trough and cold front will introduce another chance for \nshowers and thunderstorms late Monday afternoon and especially \nMonday night from west to east. The timing of this during the \nevening and overnight may mean that we end up with some leftover \nstorms from the west with decaying instability. With that said, \nthere could be enough leftover to produce some small hail and gusty \nwinds. Severe chances are looking overall small, but a rogue low-end \nsevere storm can't be totally ruled out (<2% chance).\n\nOn Tuesday, there may be a bit of a battleground between post-cold \nfrontal northwesterly flow and wherever the cold front ends up \nduring the afternoon. Some storms could be possible mainly in \nnorthwest Wisconsin with a northerly solution, but right now it's \nlooking like that threat is trending south and Tuesday will \nprobably be dry for most places. \n\nWednesday and Thursday are looking generally dry with high pressure \npassing through. Temperatures should be roughly seasonal with highs \nin the 70s to around 80. Around Friday, another cold front is \nlooking possible with potential for some showers and thunderstorms. \nStrong or severe storms look unlikely at this time. High pressure \nand quiet weather is favored as we go into next weekend.",
